# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b8), edited.

~ r01
Narrator: You catch the low murmur of a drover warming his chapped hands over a brazier of damp peat. He swears a cooper's lad up the lane opened his mouth to say grace and spoke instead in the rusted, grating baritone of a long-dead magistrate.
* Listen a while longer -> r02
* Move on -> END

~ r02
Narrator: Two women in woollen shawls trade sharp whispers over a basket of bruised turnips, their breath misting in the cold. One vows her neighbour put the evil eye on her dairy, turning a whole wheel of sweet cream cheese green and weeping with maggots.
* Listen a while longer -> r03
* Move on -> END

~ r03
Narrator: A tinker nursing a mug of sour cider leans toward the hearth, lowering his voice so the barkeep will not hear. He claims three toddlers in the outer cottages have grown coarse grey bristles along their spines and taken to gnawing raw marrow bones under the beds.
* Listen a while longer -> r04
* Move on -> END

~ r04
Narrator: By the well, a watchman rubs his aching knees and grumbles about a series of peculiar break-ins across the parish. The thief leaves silver spoons and coin purses untouched, taking only family Bibles, baby teeth from keepsake boxes, and locks of hair clipped from sleeping folk.
* Listen a while longer -> r05
* Move on -> END

~ r05
Narrator: You overhear a woodcutter telling his trembling apprentice to stay clear of the high bracken after the third bell. They found a shepherd standing upright in the gorse with his eyes wide open and cold as river stones, his chest empty of breath while something pale loped into the fog.
* Listen a while longer -> r06
* Move on -> END

~ r06
Narrator: Behind the stables, two carters pass a clay pipe back and forth, speaking of strangers with gold-leaf rings and oiled blades. Folks say a secret brotherhood of misers in the valley pays those quiet knives to slit the throats of debtors and weigh their hearts against brass scales.
* Listen a while longer -> r07
* Move on -> END

~ r07
Narrator: An old woman shelling peas on her stoop mutters to her cat about the barefoot penitents gathering up on the tors. They smear their faces with mutton tallow and ash, howling prayers to hurry the end of the world before the moon can shut her watchful eye.
* Listen a while longer -> r08
* Move on -> END

~ r08
Narrator: A gravedigger scraping mud from his spade tells the sexton about the corpse they carried in from the crossroad ditch. The dead man's hair had turned snow-white from terror, and they had to crack his stiff knuckles with a mallet to pry a cold marble finger from his fist.
* Listen a while longer -> r09
* Move on -> END

~ r09
Narrator: Two washerwomen wringing out linen sheets gossip about a wealthy widow who bolted her shutters at the start of the month. The milkman smelled brimstone and burnt sugar through her keyhole, and saw a tall suitor in velvet cuffs whose shadow wore a pair of curled horns.
* Listen a while longer -> r10
* Move on -> END

~ r10
Narrator: Near the lychgate, a choirboy whispers to his sister while tossing dry crusts onto the frost. He says the bluebirds nesting in the rafters never sing during hymns anymore, only cock their bright heads to listen when sinners confess and tap the windowpanes in three-beat riddles.
* Listen a while longer -> r11
* Move on -> END

~ r11
Narrator: A drover leaning on the paddock rail points a scarred thumb toward the black ridge beyond the moor. Every night the wind carries the reek of pine tar and scorching wool from distant bonfires, keeping time with skin drums that thud like a giant's slow heartbeat.
* Listen a while longer -> r12
* Move on -> END

~ r12
Narrator: Over the clatter of pewter plates, a seamstress tells the chandler about the teenagers who wandered back from the hazel copse. They sit in the mud braiding dead nettles into crowns, speaking only in crooked nursery rhymes that make the village hounds whine and crawl under the porch.
* Listen a while longer -> r13
* Move on -> END

~ r13
Narrator: A trapper smells of wet hound and woodsmoke as he warns the ostler away from the northern marsh. His own brother came back from the reeds without his rifle or his tongue's old cunning, only whistling four wet, reed-thin notes whenever someone speaks his name.
* Listen a while longer -> r14
* Move on -> END

~ r14
Narrator: Two weavers waiting by the fuller's vats compare the sour taste in their mouths after waking from the very same nightmare. Half the lane dreamed of climbing a ladder of braided silver frost into the black sky, where cold stars sang to them like choirs of glass.
* Listen a while longer -> r15
* Move on -> END

~ r15
Narrator: A dockhand spits into the gutter and warns a passing peddler never to buy meat pasties from the cellar bakehouse after dark. He swears he found a long, scaly tail baked into the lard crust, though the pepper gravy was rich enough to make him eat a second.
* Listen a while longer -> r16
* Move on -> END

~ r16
Narrator: Down by the weir, an angler mending his hemp net swears the river is growing crowded with things that are not trout. He saw pale, webbed hands gripping the slick stones below the millwheel, and wide yellow eyes blinking up through the brown foam.
* Listen a while longer -> r17
* Move on -> END

~ r17
Narrator: A ploughman nursing a bandaged thumb tells the smith what his iron share struck in the south fallow field. They dug up a bronze vessel green with verdigris and sealed with human teeth, and since that hour every crow in the parish has circled their chimney.
* Listen a while longer -> r18
* Move on -> END

~ r18
Narrator: Two girls stringing dried apples by the lantern light speak in hushed voices about last season's harvest queen. Her wreath of wheat and ribbons was found floating in the horse trough at dawn, and nobody has seen hide nor hair of the lass since the feast.
* Listen a while longer -> r19
* Move on -> END

~ r19
Narrator: An apothecary measuring dried rue tells his customer to cross the street before passing the crooked gable at the end of the lane. The little girl looking out that dusty casement has worn the same pinafore and held the same china doll since his grandfather was a boy.
* Listen a while longer -> r20
* Move on -> END

~ r20
Narrator: A sweep brushing soot from his sleeves nods up toward the dark belfry above the square. Last week the iron bell struck a seventh, nameless hour in the dead of night, and the old watchmaker and his wife walked hand in hand right off the parapet.
* Listen a while longer -> r01
* Move on -> END
